Title: THE CALL OF THE WILD
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER ESTATE - DAY

Sunlight warms the sprawling green lawns and wide verandas of Judge Miller's Santa Clara Valley estate.

BUCK, a massive one-hundred-and-forty-pound cross between a St. Bernard and a Scotch Shepherd, trots imperiously past fox terriers along the gravel driveway.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and with warm, long hair, from Puget Sound to San Diego. Men, groping in the Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal.

MANUEL, a lean gardener's helper with a gambling debt in his eyes, steps out from behind a grape arbor holding a loop of heavy rope. He clicks his tongue softly.

Buck approaches calmly, trusting the familiar hand. Manuel loops the thick cord around Buck's neck, beneath his brass collar.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For to play a system requires money, while the wages of a gardener's helper do not lap over the needs of a wife and numerous progeny.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK TRAIN STATION - NIGHT

A solitary lantern illuminates the small flag station. A STRANGER in a heavy coat hands a thick roll of bills to Manuel.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'em.

Manuel twists the rope tight around Buck's throat.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls in surprised outrage and leaps at the Stranger. The Stranger grapples Buck by the throat, wrestling him down and wrenching the rope tighter.

Buck chokes violently, his tongue lolling out, eyes glazing over as darkness takes him. The two men hoist his limp body into a train baggage car.

INT. WATERFRONT SHED - SAN FRANCISCO - NIGHT

A sickly tallow candle flickers in a dim, damp shed behind a saloon.

A SALOON-KEEPER hands currency to the Stranger, whose hand is bandaged in a bloody rag.

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty, and he's worth it, or I'm a squarehead. Lend me a hand before you pull your freight.

They pin Buck down on the dirt floor. The Saloon-Keeper works a heavy file across Buck's brass collar until it snaps off.

They lift Buck's dazed, gasping frame and hurl him into a wooden cage-like crate, slamming the wooden slats shut.

EXT. SEATTLE BACKYARD - DAY

Four ragged porters set the wooden crate inside a high-walled backyard.

A stout MAN IN A RED SWEATER steps out of a shed, clutching a hatchet and a heavy wooden club.

DRIVER
You ain't going to take him out now?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Sure.

The Man in Red Sweater drives the hatchet into the crate, prying off top boards.

Buck, eyes bloodshot and mane bristling, hurls his full weight against the splintering wood. The opening clears. Buck leaps in mid-air, jaws open for the man's throat.

The heavy club strikes Buck mid-flight with a crushing impact. Buck smashes into the dirt on his side.

Screaming in rage, Buck springs to his feet and charges again. The club cracks down across his head.

Again and again Buck charges; again and again the club smashes him to the frozen ground.

Blood streams from Buck's nose and ears. He staggers up, barely able to stand. The Man in Red Sweater steps forward and deals a final heavy blow to his nose. Buck collapses, unconscious.

The Man in Red Sweater lowers the club, stepping close to pat Buck's bruised head calmly.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Answers to the name of Buck. Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

He sets down a pan of fresh water and a piece of raw meat. Buck, battered and bleeding, drinks hungrily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. He had learned the lesson, and in all his after life he never forgot it. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

PERRAULT, a wiry French-Canadian courier, steps into the yard and inspects Buck's legs and chest. He turns to the Man in Red Sweater and hands over a stack of bills.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

The steamer Narwhal rolls in icy waters. Snow falls through the gray air.

PERRAULT and FRANCOIS, a swarthy half-breed driver, lead Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, onto the slippery wooden deck.

Buck steps onto the cold surface. His paws sink into the white mushy snow. He snorts, shaking his fur, then licks a dusting of white off his nose. It stings like fire and melts.

Francois chuckles, patting Buck's flank.

FRANCOIS
Look at dat Buck! First time he see de snow!

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

A chaos of tents, sleds, and shouting prospectors lines the snowy shore near a log store.

Curly walks toward a wolfish husky the size of a shepherd. Friendly, she wags her tail.

Without warning, the husky leaps in like a flash, teeth snapping shut, ripping Curly's face open from eye to jaw.

Curly rushes back, but the husky shoulders her off her feet. She rolls onto her side.

A score of starving huskies instantly swarm over her in a snapping, howling mass.

FRANCOIS
Sacredam!

Francois rushes in with an axe, while Perrault swings a heavy club, beating back the wild pack.

The swarm scatters, leaving Curly limp and lifeless in the bloody snow.

Nearby, SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with a treacherous gaze, runs out his red tongue, grinning.

Buck stares at Curly's torn body, his eyes wide with horror, then fixes a dark gaze upon Spitz.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Well, he would see to it that he never went down.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - CANYON - DAY

Francois fastens leather harnesses and traces over Buck, pairing him between DAVE, a gloomy veteran wheeler, and SOL-LEKS, an old one-eyed husky. Spitz leads at the front.

Francois cracks his whip overhead.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

The team surges forward, dragging a heavily loaded firewood sled across deep glaciers and frozen passes.

When Buck strays from the line, Dave nips his hindquarters firmly; when he pulls true, Dave relaxes.

By sunset, they pull into camp at Lake Bennett. Francois inspects Buck's sore paws by lantern light.

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

A cutting blizzard howls against a perpendicular cliff of rock. A small campfire thaws the ice beneath it.

Buck finishes his ration of dried salmon and trots back to his snug nest dug deep into the snow bank beneath the cliff face.

A low, vicious snarl greets him from inside the hole. Spitz's sharp white teeth flash from Buck's warm nest.

Buck bristles, his eyes turning red. He leaps into the snow hole, teeth bared, striking Spitz.

Suddenly, loud shouts and thumping clubs shatter the darkness.

PERRAULT
Out! Get out, you starving devils!

Scores of gaunt, skeleton-like wild huskies, eyes blazing with hunger-madness, pour into the camp. They swarm the grub-box, knocking it over and devouring the bacon.

Buck is set upon by three starving invaders at once. His head and shoulders are slashed open.

Beside him, Spitz treacherously snaps at Buck's exposed throat while Buck fights off the invaders.

Francois and Perrault charge in with heavy clubs, laying about with frantic blows until the wild pack flees into the timber.

Francois examines the bleeding team by the embers of the fire.

FRANCOIS
Ah, my frien's, mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites. What you t'ink, eh, Perrault?

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

Full moonlight bathes the frozen river bed in silver.

A SNOWSHOE RABBIT flashes across the snow like a pale frost wraith.

Buck leads a pack of sixty dogs in full cry, his body flashing forward leap by leap, whining with the blood-lust of the hunt.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All that stirring of old instincts which at stated periods drives men out to kill--all this was Buck's, only it was infinitely more intimate. He was running the wild thing down, to kill with his own teeth and wash his muzzle in warm blood.

Up ahead, Spitz leaps down from an overhanging bank, cutting off the bend. His white teeth snap the rabbit's spine in mid-air. The rabbit shrieks.

Buck doesn't check his stride. He hurls himself straight at Spitz, shoulder to shoulder.

Both dogs roll over and over in the powdery snow. Spitz leaps clear, teeth clicking like a steel trap.

The rest of the pack halts, forming a silent, expectant circle in the moonlight.

Spitz counters every strike, gashing Buck's neck and shoulders. Buck bleeds heavily, panting, but his eyes remain sharp.

Buck feints high for the throat, then sweeps low to the snow. His jaws snap shut on Spitz's left foreleg. A crunch of bone echoes across the silence.

Spitz staggers onto three legs. Buck rushes again, sweeping low to break the right foreleg.

Spitz quivers, struggling madly to stand, but loses his balance. Buck leaps in, shoulder striking shoulder.

Spitz disappears beneath the closing circle of the grey pack.

Buck stands tall in the center of the snowfield, his head high, chest blood-stained.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - MORNING

Francois brings Sol-leks to the front of the sled to take the lead position.

Buck springs upon Sol-leks, driving him back and standing squarely in the leader's spot.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job. Go 'way, Chook!

Francois grabs Buck by the scruff and drags him back, putting Sol-leks ahead.

Buck circles out of reach of Francois's club, growling softly but resolutely. He refuses to take his old position behind Dave.

Perrault checks his pocket watch impatiently.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club. We lose de time!

Francois sighs and tosses the club into the snow. He unfastens Sol-leks and points to the lead.

FRANCOIS
All right, Buck. Come on den.

Buck trots in, swinging into position at the head of the line. Francois hooks the traces.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

Buck surges forward with tremendous power. The whole team leaps as one dog behind him.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Months of relentless travel have worn the team to skin and bone. Buck's weight is down; his coat is dull and matted.

A SCOTCH HALF-BREED driver halts the mail train in front of a line of supply stores.

HAL, a callow youth wearing a massive revolver belt, and CHARLES, a weak-faced older man, hand a bag of money to the Scotch Half-Breed.

MERCEDES, Charles's spoiled, fluttery wife, stands beside a mountain of impractical baggage--canned goods, clothes-sacks, and a heavy wall-tent.

HAL
Mush on, you lazy brutes! Mush!

Hal slams a thick whip down across Buck's raw back.

The exhausted dogs strain against the breast-bands, but the frozen runners will not budge from the ice.

A BYSTANDER stepped off the boardwalk, shaking his head.

BYSTANDER
They're plum tuckered out, mister. And the runners are froze fast. Break 'em out first.

Hal grunts, prying the gee-pole side to side until the runners crack loose. He whips Buck again.

HAL
Mush!

Buck digs his claws into the packed snow, dragging the top-heavy load forward down the sloping street.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - WHITE RIVER - SPRING DAY

Sunlight glints off rotten, slushy river ice. Water trickles through cracks.

At the river bank, JOHN THORNTON, a rugged, weathered woodsman, sits on a log whittling a birch axe-handle.

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes stagger up with five emaciated dogs. Buck collapses into the snow the moment the sled stops, his ribs outlined clearly through loose skin.

THORNTON
They told you up above the bottom was dropping out of the trail. Only fools would risk that ice.

HAL
All the same, we'll go on to Dawson. Get up there, Buck! Hi! Mush on!

Hal lashes Buck with the whip. Buck stays down, his eyes glazed, numb to the pain.

Hal drops the whip and picks up a thick wooden club, raining heavy blows onto Buck's head and ribs.

Thornton drops his whittling, utters a wild, animal-like cry, and hurls himself onto Hal, knocking him backward into the slush.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you!

Hal draws his long hunting-knife. Thornton strikes Hal's knuckles sharply with the birch axe-handle, sending the knife flying into the snow.

Thornton stoops, picks up the knife, and cuts Buck's traces in two quick strokes.

Hal clears his bloody mouth, turns his back, and grabs the gee-pole.

HAL
Get up, Pike! Sol-leks! Mush!

The four remaining dogs stagger onto the sagging river ice, dragging the heavy sled and Mercedes.

Thornton kneels by Buck, gently feeling his sides.

A quarter-mile out, the rear of the sled drops suddenly. Mercedes screams.

Charles turns back, but the entire section of ice gives way. Humans, dogs, and sled disappear into the dark, roaring current. A gaping black hole is all that remains.

Thornton looks at the hole, then down at Buck.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck raises his head weakly and licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - SUMMER DAY

A cozy spruce-bough lodge sits near a sparkling mountain stream.

SKEET, a little Irish setter, licks the healing sores on Buck's broad shoulder as Buck lies resting in the warm grass.

Thornton approaches, leaning down to take Buck's massive head roughly between his hands, resting his forehead against Buck's nose.

THORNTON
You lazy old rascal. You dirty dog.

Buck closes his jaws gently over Thornton's hand, pressing without breaking the skin.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON - STREET - WINTER DAY

Sixty below zero. A crowd of hundreds of fur-clad miners surrounds a sled loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour--one thousand pounds total.

MATTHEWSON, a wealthy Bonanza King, slaps a sack of gold dust onto a wood barrel.

MATTHEWSON
A thousand dollars says he can't start it and walk off with it for a hundred yards!

Thornton turns to JIM O'BRIEN, an old comrade.

THORNTON
Can you lend me a thousand?

O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I have the beast can do the trick, John.

O'Brien drops his gold sack beside Matthewson's.

Thornton kneels beside Buck in the freezing snow. He rests cheek to cheek against Buck's head.

THORNTON
(whispering)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines softly, seizing Thornton's mittened hand in his jaws, then letting go.

Thornton steps back ten yards.

THORNTON
Now, Buck!

Buck pulls the traces taut, then slacks them back a few inches.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings sharp to the right, plunging forward. The frozen runners crackle softly.

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck lunges to the left. The ice bonds snap; the sled pivots two inches.

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his whole one-hundred-and-fifty-pound frame forward. His chest lowers to the snow; his claws dig deep parallel grooves into the ice.

The sled sways, trembles, and lurches forward--an inch... three inches... half a foot.

The crowd holds its breath. Buck's muscles writhe under his thick fur as he catches the momentum, hauling the heavy sled steadily down the street.

He passes the hundred-yard firewood post. The crowd breaks into a roaring cheer, throwing hats and mittens into the freezing air.

Thornton falls to his knees beside Buck, hugging his neck tightly, tears streaming down his face.

THORNTON
You beauty! You magnificent devil!

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
I'll give you a thousand for him, sir! Twelve hundred!

THORNTON
Sir, you can go to hell. That's the best I can do for you.

EXT. VIRGIN EAST VALLEY - DAY

Golden autumn leaves blanket a remote, shallow creek valley. Spruce-bough shelters stand near piles of filled moose-hide gold sacks.

Buck sits on his haunches at the edge of the forest, ears pricked.

From deep within the dark timber echoes a long, mournful wolf howl.

Buck rises, his mane bristling. He bounds silently into the deep woods.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - DAY

Buck glides cat-footed through the shadows.

Ahead, a long, lean TIMBER WOLF stands on his haunches, howling up at the sky.

The wolf senses Buck and turns to run. Buck chases him down a dry creek bed, cornering him against a log jam.

The wolf whirled, snarling defensively. Buck does not attack; he circles slowly, tail stiff, making friendly overtures.

Caution turns to trust. The wolf advances, and they sniff noses warmly, playing together in the moss.

The wolf lopes off into the forest, looking back for Buck to follow.

Buck trots alongside him for a mile, wild and free. Then he halts. The image of John Thornton fills his mind.

Buck turns back toward camp. The wild wolf sits on his haunches and howls mournfully as Buck retreats.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - CLEARING - DAY

Buck emerges from the forest at a rapid lope, carrying a fresh scent on the wind. He stops dead.

NIG lies on his side in the grass, a feathered arrow piercing his body.

A hundred yards ahead, HANS lies face down, bristling with arrows like a porcupine.

From the ruined camp comes the sound of triumph--YEEHAT INDIANS dancing and chanting around the burning wreckage of the spruce-bough lodge.

A roar of terrifying ferocity erupts from Buck's throat.

Buck transforms into a hurricane of fury, hurling himself into the center of the camp.

He strikes the Yeehat Chief, ripping his throat wide open in passing, then leaps instantly to a second hunter, tearing his jugular before he can raise his spear.

Panic strikes the Yeehats.

YEEHAT HUNTER
The Evil Spirit! The Ghost Dog!

They shoot arrows wildly, hitting each other in the chaos, and flee in terror into the forest with Buck raging at their heels, dragging them down into the moss.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - POOL - EVENING

Silence returns to the desolated clearing.

Buck walks slowly to the edge of a deep, muddy river pool. SKEET lies dead at the water's edge.

Buck sniffs the water. Thornton's scent leads straight into the dark depth, with no trail leading out.

Buck sits on his haunches by the pool, letting out a low, aching moan.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. It left a great void in him, somewhat akin to hunger, but a void which ached and ached, and which food could not fill. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - CLEARING - NIGHT

A full moon rises high, bathing the valley in ghostly silver light.

From the surrounding hills, a chorus of sharp yelps approaches.

A silvery flood of timber wolves pours out of the dark trees into the clearing.

They halt, awed by the massive, motionless creature standing before them.

A bold wolf leaps straight for Buck. Like lightning, Buck strikes, snapping the attacker's neck in a single bite.

Three more charge in succession; Buck slashes their throats, sending them back dripping blood.

The pack surges forward en masse. Buck whippers and pivots, his jaws snapping, backing into a right-angle gravel bank formed by the miners' pit.

Protected on three sides, he stands at bay, driving back every assault until the pack retreats, winded and bleeding.

A gaunt, battle-scarred old wolf steps forward cautiously, sniffing. Buck lowers his snarl and sniffs back.

It is his WILD BROTHER from the forest. They touch noses.

The Old Wolf sits on his haunches, points his muzzle to the glowing moon, and raises the long wolf howl.

The pack joins in the chorus.

Buck sits back, raises his massive head to the stars, and lets loose his own deep, wild wolf howl, echoing across the silent mountains.

The leaders leap away into the dark forest.

Buck springs out from the gravel bank and runs alongside his wild brother, leaping gigantic at the head of the pack, singing the song of the younger world into the Arctic night.

FADE OUT.
